<!-- The problem — what each of the two paths gives up. In flow mode, two cards in a `grow="1"` grid
     take all the space below the title. Inside each card, top to bottom: the path's name (kicker), a
     small picture mimicking that path's output, a list, and a verdict. The verdict line sits at the
     bottom of the card via `justify="between"` — both verdicts must line up at the same height for
     "one or the other" to read side by side.

     Each list item is one line — the cards must fit below the title even in a theme with a large
     heading face (editorial). The small pictures are evidence that shows instead of tells. On the
     left, a single picture of a slide (hatched fill = solid pixels, with a file-name tag centered on
     top); on the right, code that writes coordinates by hand. The verdict lines that name the loss
     are painted in `accent5` (red) alone — on this slide red means only "you lose this".

     The circle in the middle can't sit between two grid cells without a `<stack>`, so the whole card
     row is wrapped in a stack and the circle is placed on top with `at="center"`. -->
<slide id="problem" enter="rise">
  <text variant="kicker">Why another tool</text>
  <h1>Slides as text force a trade-off</h1>
  <stack w="fill" grow="1">
    <grid cols="2" gap="2xl" w="fill" h="fill">
      <shape variant="card" type="rounded-rect" w="fill" h="fill" pad="lg">
        <col gap="md" h="fill" justify="between">
          <col gap="md">
            <text variant="kicker" color="muted">Markdown-based · Marp · Slidev</text>
            <stack w="fill" h="76">
              <shape type="rounded-rect" fill="pattern(wide-diagonal-up muted/35 bg/0)" border="1 dashed muted" radius="sm" w="fill" h="fill"/>
              <shape type="rounded-rect" fill="bg" border="none" radius="sm" pad="xs sm" at="center">
                <text role="code" size="17" color="muted">slide-03.png · one image</text>
              </shape>
            </stack>
            <ul size="22">
              <li>Document markup, no slide vocabulary</li>
              <li>PPTX export is a single image</li>
            </ul>
          </col>
          <text role="heading" size="28" color="accent5">✕ You lose the editable output</text>
        </col>
      </shape>
      <shape variant="card" type="rounded-rect" w="fill" h="fill" pad="lg">
        <col gap="md" h="fill" justify="between">
          <col gap="md">
            <text variant="kicker" color="muted">Library-based · python-pptx</text>
            <shape type="rounded-rect" fill="surface" border="none" radius="sm" w="fill" h="76" pad="xs sm">
              <pre size="16">box.left = Emu(1219200)  # measured by hand
box.top  = Emu(2286000)</pre>
            </shape>
            <ul size="22">
              <li>Every element placed by hand in EMU</li>
              <li>No design API, so you assemble XML</li>
            </ul>
          </col>
          <text role="heading" size="28" color="accent5">✕ You lose ease of writing</text>
        </col>
      </shape>
    </grid>
    <shape type="ellipse" fill="bg" border="2 solid surface" shadow="soft" w="64" h="64" at="center" enter="zoom">
      <text role="heading" size="22" color="muted" align="center" anchor="center">vs</text>
    </shape>
  </stack>
  <note>You lose one or the other: expressiveness, or an editable output. balpyo aims for the spot where you keep both.</note>
</slide>
